\title{GeoZarr "multiscales" convention}
\description{
This class implements the "multiscales" convention. The
convention is placed in the attributes of a Zarr group and lists the nodes
below that group that hold the same data at different resolutions, with for
each such level the level that it was derived from.

The convention is registered via the following CMO:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{\{
  "schema_url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zarr-conventions/multiscales/refs/tags/v0.1/schema.json",
  "spec_url": "https://github.com/zarr-conventions/multiscales/blob/v0.1/README.md",
  "uuid": "d35379db-88df-4056-af3a-620245f8e347",
  "name": "multiscales",
  "description": "Multiscale layout of zarr datasets"
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The \code{multiscales} object has the following structure:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{
  "multiscales": \{
    "layout": [
      \{ "asset": "0",
        "spatial:shape": [1200, 1200],
        "spatial:transform": [10.0, 0.0, 500000.0, 0.0, -10.0, 5000000.0] \},
      \{ "asset": "1",
        "derived_from": "0",
        "transform": \{ "scale": [2.0, 2.0], "translation": [0.0, 0.0] \},
        "spatial:shape": [600, 600],
        "spatial:transform": [20.0, 0.0, 500000.0, 0.0, -20.0, 5000000.0] \}
    ],
    "resampling_method": "average"
  \}
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Assets.} An \code{asset} is a path relative to the group holding the
\code{multiscales} object. It may resolve to a group or to an array; the
attributes do not tell which, that follows from the node itself.

\strong{Scale.} The \code{scale} of a level is relative to the level named in its
\code{derived_from}, not to the full-resolution level. The levels thus form a
tree and the scale relative to the root of that tree is the product of the
scales on the path to the root, see \code{cumulative_scale()}. A \code{transform} on
a level without \code{derived_from} has no meaning and is ignored.

\strong{What this class does not interpret.} The specification does not define
to which dimensions the entries of \code{scale} and \code{translation} apply, nor in
what order, nor the units of \code{translation}. This class therefore stores
these properties but never derives georeferencing from them. The
\code{spatial:transform} and \code{spatial:shape} of a level, when present, are the
authoritative description of its grid. Levels added with \code{add_level()}
always have a zero \code{translation}.

Registering this convention with \code{register()} also registers the "spatial"
convention when any level has \verb{spatial:} properties. Call \code{register()}
after the levels have been set.
}
\section{Interpretation}{

Arrays in the levels of a multiscales group that uses the "spatial"
convention are opened as \code{geozarr_array}. The specification is ambiguous or
silent on several points; this implementation reads it as follows:
\itemize{
\item Interpretation:
\itemize{
\item An \code{asset} may be a group or an array; which one follows from the node, not from the attributes. An array takes part in a level when the "spatial" convention is declared on the multiscales group or on the array, and both names in \code{spatial:dimensions} are among its \code{dimension_names}. Other arrays in a level group, such as coordinate arrays, are left as plain Zarr arrays.
\item \code{scale} is relative to the level in \code{derived_from}, not to the full-resolution level. \code{cumulative_scale()} gives the product along \code{derived_from} to the root. A \code{transform} on a level without \code{derived_from} is ignored there.
\item \code{scale} and \code{translation} are never used for georeferencing, because the specification does not define the dimensions they apply to, their order, or the units of \code{translation}. The grid of a level comes from its \code{spatial:transform}.
\item \verb{spatial:} and \verb{proj:} attributes are taken, in order, from the array, its parent group, the layout object of the level, and the multiscales group. Attributes of the multiscales group thus reach arrays that are not its direct children, and a \code{spatial:transform} on the multiscales group never overrides that of a level. Arrays in a level need not declare a convention themselves.
\item The multiscales layout takes precedence over XArray-style coordinate arrays in a level group.
}
\item Stricter than the schema:
\itemize{
\item The layout is rejected when an asset is listed more than once, when a \code{derived_from} does not name an asset in the layout, when the \code{derived_from} references form a cycle, or when \code{scale} arrays differ in length. A rejected \code{multiscales} attribute is ignored with a warning; the store still opens.
\item A \code{scale} that is not the ratio of the cell sizes in the \code{spatial:transform} of a level and its source gives a warning on reading and an error in \code{add_level()}.
\item The convention must be declared in \code{zarr_conventions}. Stores citing the \code{v1} tags are not recognised.
\item Writing with \code{add_level()}:
\item A level must be added after its source. \code{scale} is required with \code{derived_from}, \code{translation} is always zero, and no \code{transform} is written for a level without \code{derived_from}.
\item Unknown properties of layout objects are preserved; other properties of the \code{multiscales} object are not.
}
\item Not supported:
\itemize{
\item Levels without their own \code{spatial:transform}. Such a level takes the \code{spatial:transform} of the multiscales group when there is one, which is wrong for all but the full-resolution level; otherwise opening the store fails.
\item A multiscales group without the "spatial" convention: the layout is available but the arrays are plain Zarr arrays.
\item A non-zero \code{translation}.
}
}
}

